  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nThe main forecast issue today will be the warm and dry\nconditions Thursday resulting in fire weather concerns for much\nof the forecast area. Will discuss that in more detail in the\nFire Weather section below. \n\nIt's a little breezy in some areas, but overall a very nice\nafternoon is in store. Highs will be mainly in the 70s. Cool and\nquiet conditions are expected tonight with low in the mid 40s to\nlower 50s.\n\nA system well to our north, in central Canada, will swing a cold\nfront through the areas Thursday night into Friday. Ahead of\nthis front, we are expecting very dry and warm air to move into\nwestern and central North Dakota on Thursday. Afternoon highs\nwill be well into the 80s south and west of the Missouri River\nand mainly lower to mid 80s east. The Turtle Mountains area will\nsee cooler conditions in the mid to upper 70s. The very dry\nconditions will produce critical to near critical fire weather\nconditions for all but the far northern and far eastern portions\nof the Forecast Area.\n\nThe cold front drop south through the area Thursday night into\nFriday morning. Western and north central portions of the state\nwill experience noticeably cooler temperatures with highs in mid\n60 along the International Border, to the mid 70s over the far\nsouthwest. The southern James River Valley will see high similar\nto today, in the mid 80s. \n\nThe cold front will be a dry front, but eventually some\nincreasing humidity will drop south into the state. Combined\nwith an impulse tracking through a quasi-zonal flow, scattered\nshowers are possible Friday, currently the northwest and parts\nof the north central are forecast to see low to medium (20 to\n60%) chances for showers with low qpf amounts of only a few \nhundredths most areas. \n\nA digging upper level low off the Pacific Northwest coast on\nFriday will then bring our next chances for showers and\nthunderstorms over the weekend and into early next week. A lot\nof uncertainty remains at this time, but we may see a couple\nround of precipitation chances from later Saturday through early\nMonday before it lifts into southern portions of Canada on\nMonday. Temperatures remain cool (mainly in the 60s) through \nthe weekend and into early next week. Sunday could be quite raw \nwith low 60s and some 50 degree reading under cloudy skies \nstrong easterly surface winds.",
